A||||7||Reckard||Sunroof
Z||000000||Reckard||06-30-99||10:36 AM||||Has anyone replaced the gasket in the factory sunroof.  If so what is the procedure.  The dealer here along with the other body shops wont replace it.
Z||000001||theogre||06-30-99||07:09 PM||||First, get the new one from GM. I don't have the part number, but I'm told it's available.<p>I think the seal is glued to the framing. If it is then you have to remove the old one, yes you'll destroy it and make a mess, Then you have to clean the frame completely. You then glue the new one down with an adhesive made specifically for attaching body seals. You can get it at most parts stores. DO NOT substitute another adhesive, it won't work.<p>If it's not glued, and I'd be suprised if it's not, then it just clips onto the frame. I would not be suprised if it was glued and had clips inside it. The trunk seal is like that on mine.<p>I haven't gotten the far with my roof yet.... it doesn't leak.<p>If the thing's not totally cracked up, and before you go to all the trouble to replace it, try cleanig it really good. "Basin, Tub, & Tile" type cleaner works good on it. After you clean it treat it with armor all, or some similar not greasy type protectant. Dirt on the seal will make it leak, and it just looks like hell.<p>While we're on the subject, for those who have 84 and other years, where the edges of the glass coating are looking cruddy..... GM used a differant glass on the later years that is painted around the edges. It holds up much better than the early glass which often has seal scrubs thru the tint coating.<p>Q. Acording to my notes, GM made a shade pannel for the vista roofs. Has anyone ever seen one? I've seen dozens of Fiero's over the years, but I've never seen the pannel.
Z||000002||Cooter||07-01-99||12:26 AM||||Ogre,<br>You asked about the shade for the roof, I have a friend who bought a 85 parts car and it had the shade in it. Unfortunatly, he wont give it to me. Keep looking
Z||000003||Reckard||07-01-99||05:56 AM||||I will definitly try cleaning it well before replacing the seal.  Also, is there a way to adjust the latch.  It doesn't seem very tight.
Z||000004||Carrolles||07-01-99||10:22 AM||||Reckard,<p>Are you replacing the sunroof seal because it is torn up or simply because the roof leaks?  The reason I ask is that the vast majority of sunroof leaks are caused by the mounting bolt gaskets not the actual sunroof seal.  That was the case with mine.<br>Ed Parks at the Fiero Factory alerted me to this.  The bolts that hold the hinge plates and the latch to the glass have rubber seals that shrink and crack.  It isn't really obvious until you remove them from the glass.  Water seaps through the hinge mounting bolts and  gets into the front chanel and eventually leaks on your shoulder and lap.<br>Take a look at this first before you go to all the trouble of replacing the sunroof seal.  Bye the way the sunroof seal is glued in place.<br>The new bolts for the glass are still available from GM and aren't very expensive.  They have the new rubber seal already attached. <br>Also take a look at the hinge plates that are bolted to the front of the glass.  They originally had a plastic coating on them.  This dries up and cracks and falls off leaving only the bare metal.  The coating added thickness to the hinge plate which helped it pull down tight against the roof seal.  When it is gone the glass does not seal to the roof gasket very well anymore.  These are also still available from GM.  I replaced all the bolts and the hinge plates for around $15.00 US.  It solved the leaks and rattles.  You can also buy the spacer plate that is found between the latch mechanism and the roof.  If needed add a second one to tighten up the latch.  I bought one to do just that but didn't need it after I replaced the hinge plates.<br>Hope this helps,<br>Carroll
Z||000005||Reckard||07-01-99||11:01 AM||||Carrolles<p>The seal is not cracked and actually looks pretty good.  The bolt seals may be leaking but after a rain you can open the sunroof and see water laying on the seal.  I will try all these suggestions before I replace the seal.  I have even considere siliconing it shut as a last resort.<p>thanks<p>Reckard
Z||000006||RossT||07-01-99||10:35 PM||||about the sunroof shade,  My 88gt sunroof<br>car, came with the shade.  It matches the<br>headliner and is stored in its own padded<br>vinly case with the words FIERO printed<br>on one side of it. I have never installed<br>it, but it does appear to be well made and<br>not likely to fall.<p>Ro22
Z||000007||GREENDANO||07-03-99||07:15 AM||||Are you guys refering to the bag that holds the sunroof?  I didn't think that was OEM, I thought Rodney Dickman made those, or was he just making replacements for originals?  If you're talking about the sunroof air deflector, I have one and am willing to sell it.  E-mail me if you're interested, I picked it up at a pick-and-pull with a MINT sunroof glass last summer.  The glass is on my car, but I have the old one too if anyone needs that.  <br> Dan-O
